findings.body:
The PR description lists "Behavioral tests for move up/down menu options" and a "Test verifying prefix items cannot be moved". The diff adds only four `toMatchSnapshot()` render tests in the `array` describe block. None of them open the menu, click Move up or Move down, exercise drag-end, or assert that a prefix item cannot move.

The snapshots confirm that the DOM contains a handle, and the lack of a handle on prefix rows. They do not show that the ordering is correct, so the reorder bug above passed without a failing test. Treat these snapshots as rendering checks only, and add real behavioral tests for reorder, menu moves, and prefix immobility.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form.snapshot.test.tsx" lines="489-566"}

The new snapshots contain generated IDs such as `DndDescribedBy-0`, `DndDescribedBy-1`, `DndDescribedBy-2` and `DndLiveRegion-N`. These are created by dnd-kit with a module-level counter, not per component instance. Each snapshot's expected IDs therefore depend on how many `DndContext` instances were rendered earlier in the same file.

Running one test in isolation, for example with `-t`, or adding or reordering tests before these, will change the IDs and fail the snapshot. Updating the snapshots blindly would also hide real markup changes. Consider mocking or stubbing the id generation for these tests, or asserting only on the handle attributes instead of the full DOM.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/__snapshots__/schema-form.snapshot.test.tsx.snap" lines="89-89"}

The drag handle button uses `focus:outline-none` and provides no visible focus style. Keyboard navigation is one of the stated features (Tab to the handle, then Space and arrow keys), so a keyboard user gets no visual cue about which handle is focused.

The rest of the schema form and the UI kit use `focus-visible:ring-1 focus-visible:ring-ring` on interactive controls, so this breaks the local pattern too. Replace `focus:outline-none` with the `focus-visible:` ring classes used by the menu trigger in the same file's snapshot.

